  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Another frosty morning, especially inland but\nmilder mornings are just around the corner. Low-level \nthicknesses increasing supports steady warming mid to late week,\nand even though high pressure will remain in place, temps will \nwarm to above climo with 70s inland. For the OBX, highs will be \ntempered by light northeast breezes off the cooler waters of the\nLabrador Current as water temps have cooled to the upper 50s.\nFor tonight lows may again be near freezing in our coldest\nspots, but over the next few nights we won't be nearly as cold\nwith lows in the 40s and 50s.  \n\nIn addition to moderating temperatures, we will remain dry with\nhigh pressure settling overhead by the end of the week, and off\nthe coast by Saturday. Fire weather concerns may grow with the \ncuring vegetation combined with low RH values each afternoon. \nLuckily winds will not be an issue with a light gradient in \nplace. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The potential for a coastal low to develop and \ngain strength off the FL coast late this week in response to a \nshortwave digging across the Gulf above SFC coastal troughing \nongoing along the FL leg of the Gulfstream continues to grow. \nHowever, what happens once a pressure center presents itself \nremains fairly uncertain with the typical questions for a day r+\nlow off the coast: where the system will track, how much it \ndeepens, how fast is it moving, etc... Initially a generally \nNward motion is expected. Two scenarios that would lead to more \nimpactful solutions are one, a faster but weaker low approaching\nthe SC coast and then passing over or even W of the FA before \nexiting the MidAtlantic coast overnight SUN. This solution would\nresult in more convective rain and a higher thunderstorm \nprobability. The second progression is a slower one, with a \ndeeper low remaining offshore. This would lead to stronger winds\nand greater coastal implications but with storm-total rainfall \naccumulations an order of magnitude less and significantly less \ntstorm chances than scenario 1. We will continue to monitor the \nsystem, as large waves and associated coastal impacts are \nbecoming more likely along the Outer Banks. 0Z guidance still\nshows both tracks on the table but its worth nothing the 0Z CMC\nis now much closer to the coast, vs. the out to sea track the\npast few days. At the same time EC-AIFS which has been \nconsistent with a track right over our area (and a deeper \nstorm), has trended toward the 0Z GFS with a track west of our \narea or more inland. Bottom line even though we are a day \ncloser, nothing has been ironed out! \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...King Tide Cycle starts second half of this \nweekend, peaking middle of next week. This higher than normal \nastronomical tide cycle is slightly stronger than the one that \noccurred the last week of OCT. Will have to see how the system \nin Key Message 3 progresses before we can determine what sort of\neffect this King Tide cycle will have on our coast.",
